1️⃣ What is TreeMap?

TreeMap is a Map implementation in Java.
It stores key-value pairs.
Automatically sorts keys in natural order (or using a Comparator).
Does NOT allow null keys, but allows multiple null values.

Internally, it is implemented as a Red-Black Tree → a self-balancing binary search tree.

✅ Key idea: Keys are always sorted.

2️⃣ Declaration & Basic Usage
import java.util.*;

public class TreeMapExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // TreeMap with Integer keys and String values
        Map<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();

        map.put(3, "Jay");
        map.put(1, "Vaishnavi");
        map.put(2, "Gayatri");
        map.put(4, "Anushka");

        System.out.println("TreeMap: " + map);

        // Get value
        System.out.println("Value for key 2: " + map.get(2));

        // Remove key
        map.remove(3);
        System.out.println("After removing key 3: " + map);

        // Iterating over entries
        System.out.println("Iterating entries:");
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, String> entry : map.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + " -> " + entry.getValue());
        }
    }
}


Output:

TreeMap: {1=Vaishnavi, 2=Gayatri, 3=Jay, 4=Anushka}
Value for key 2: Gayatri
After removing key 3: {1=Vaishnavi, 2=Gayatri, 4=Anushka}
Iterating entries:
1 -> Vaishnavi
2 -> Gayatri
4 -> Anushka


✅ Notice how the keys are automatically sorted in ascending order.

3️⃣ TreeMap Features
Feature	TreeMap
Maintains order	✅ Sorted keys (natural/comparator)
Allows null key	❌ No null key
Allows null values	✅ Multiple
Performance	O(log n) for add/get/remove
Internal Structure	Red-Black Tree (self-balancing BST)
Use-case	Sorted map, range queries

4️⃣ Custom Sorting with Comparator
Map<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());

map.put(3, "Jay");
map.put(1, "Vaishnavi");
map.put(2, "Gayatri");

System.out.println(map);


Output:

{3=Jay, 2=Gayatri, 1=Vaishnavi}


✅ Now keys are sorted descending using Comparator.

5️⃣ Differences: TreeMap vs HashMap vs LinkedHashMap

Feature	       HashMap	    LinkedHashMap	  TreeMap
Key Order	      No	     Insertion	       Sorted (natural/comparator)
Null Key	      Yes	     Yes	           No
Null Values	      Yes	     Yes	           Yes
Performance	 O(1) average	O(1) average	  O(log n)
Use-case	    General	   Maintain order	  Sorted map